comments:
In my use case I am testing against storybook stories and I would love to structure the output of the screenshots to match that of the stories to make them easier to find. Currently, I have a single test file that loops through all the stories so all the screenshots are thrown into a single directory making them hard to find. Controlling the output at the `test`/`expect` level would be ideal that way we'd have access to the `testInfo` to use to determine the directory if necessary.

This issue (https://github.com/americanexpress/jest-image-snapshot/issues/97) is similar to this request, though less flexible than I propose, which allows a top-level config option (i.e. `customDiffDir`) to set the directory where the images are to be stored. This still lumps them all into a single file just different than the top level. But the reasoning behind the feature is similar IMHO.

Using `page.screenshot({ path })` only stores the screenshot to a given directory but this image is _never_ used as the baseline image for `toMatchSnapshot` comparison. So if I try doing something like...

```ts
// foo.spec.ts
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

const myStories = [{ path: 'components/button' }];

myStories.forEach(({ path }) => {
  test('renders correctly', async ({ page }, info) => {
    await page.goto('https://my-site.com');
    const name = `${info.title}.png`;
    expect(await page.screenshot({ path: `foo.spec.ts-snapshots/${path}/${name}` })).toMatchSnapshot(name);
  });
});
```

After running the test I would get something like...

```
foo.spec.ts-snapshots/
├── basic-test-Chrome-linux.png
├── basic-test-Safari-linux.png
└── components/
    └── button/
        └── basic\ test.png
```

Where only the `./basic-test-*-linux.png` files are used for comparison and `components/button/*` files are virtually ignored.

## Solution idea

Add `path` to the `toMatchSnapshot` options.

Adding `path` to the options could allow the user to set a `path` to the screenshot for both writing and reading the baseline file. In the example above that would look something like...

```diff
myStories.forEach(({ path }) => {
  test('renders correctly', async ({ page }, info) => {
    await page.goto('https://my-site.com');
    const name = `${info.title}.png`;
-    expect(await page.screenshot({ path: `foo.spec.ts-snapshots/${path}/${name}` })).toMatchSnapshot(name);
+    expect(await page.screenshot()).toMatchSnapshot(name, { path });
  });
});
```

The output would then look something like...

```
foo.spec.ts-snapshots/
└── components/
    └── button/
        ├── basic-test-Chrome-linux.png
        └── basic-test-Safari-linux.png
```

> Note the file is still within the `foo.spec.ts-snapshots` directory for the given test, not sure how important this is but would be good to avoid collisions. Also note the file names are still processed as before into dash cased including the `project.name` and `snapshotSuffix`.

---

Pertinent code link

https://github.com/microsoft/playwright/blob/1db41c330a7d50c1264da4a7b175861b701a105e/src/test/workerRunner.ts#L247-L259

> Note the `sanitizeForFilePath` which converts screenshot names from `components/button/basic.png` to `components-button-basic.png`.
